WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.710
Our hands are incredible.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:07.350
We use them to manipulate objects and to sense
the world around us.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:13.400
That means the ideal prosthetic hand should
be able to move and feel at the same time.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.670
This robotic hand was constructed by researchers
from Cornell University

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.650
to provide a sophisticated sense of touch.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:27.760
Its soft construction makes it more sensitive
to the texture and hardness of objects.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:34.920
A sensor embedded in the fingers uses optics
to convey information about pressure, texture, and position

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:40.840
Researchers tested the robotic hand by asking it to pick out the ripest tomato.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.931
It gently presses on each one, just as you
would in the supermarket.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:52.830
Then it finds the softest one and picks it
out.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:59.820
It can also run its “fingers” along an
object to determine its shape and texture.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.400
This is just the prototype, but its simple
materials and 3D printed design

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.750
should make it easy to manufacture.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.310
This handsome robot could make life easier
for a lot of people.

